MOUNT_PRODOS(8)      UNIX Programmer's Manual      MOUNT_PRODOS(8)



NAME
     mount_prodos - mount a ProDOS volume

SYNOPSIS
     mount_prodos [ -o options ] special node

DESCRIPTION
     Mount_prodos attaches the ProDOS volume on the block device
     special to the directory node.  It is not normally run by
     hand: mount(8) runs it for "mount -t prodos".  The options
     are mount(8)'s -o list.

     The volume is read-only unless -o write is given.  A mount
     asked for read-write without it is made read-only, and
     mount_prodos says so:

   /dev/sd1 on /mnt: writing needs -o write, mounted read-only.

     Asked with -r (or -o ro) it is quiet.  A writable mount the
     kernel refuses is retried read-only and reported as
     "Read-only file system, mounted read-only".  A device that is
     not a ProDOS volume is reported as "not a ProDOS volume".

OPTIONS
     -o write  mount the volume writable.  Files can be written,
               extended, truncated, created, removed and renamed,
               and directories made, removed and renamed.  The
               writing half of the file system is the module
               prodosw; without it loaded, a writable mount is
               refused and made read-only.

NAMES
     A ProDOS name is 1 to 15 characters of A-Z, 0-9 and ".",
     starting with a letter.  Lookup ignores case, as ProDOS and
     GS/OS do, so Casper, CASPER and casper are one file.  A name
     that no ProDOS volume could hold is refused with EINVAL.

     A directory lists each name in the case GS/OS recorded for it
     (GS/OS Technical Note #8), and in lowercase where no case was
     recorded.  A file or directory created here records the case
     of the name it was created with.

FORKED FILES
     A GS/OS extended file has a data fork and a resource fork.
     NAME reads and writes its data fork.  Beside it is ._NAME, an
     AppleDouble version 2 file (RFC 1740) holding the file's
     Finder information and then its resource fork, as macOS and
     netatalk store one on a filesystem without extended
     attributes.  The Finder information is the volume's own when
     the file carries it, and otherwise what GS/OS's HFS file
     system translator makes of the ProDOS file type and auxiliary
     type: creator "pdos", and type "p" followed by the file type
     and auxiliary type unless the translator's table names one.
     A file with no resource fork has no ._NAME, and ._NAME is
     never written, created or removed: opening one with O_CREAT
     or unlinking, renaming or truncating it fails with EPERM.
     Unlinking NAME removes both forks, renaming it renames both,
     and truncating it truncates the data fork.

PERMISSIONS
     ProDOS has no owners.  Every file and directory is owned by
     user 0 and group 0.

     The mode comes from the ProDOS access byte:

          $01  read    r for owner, group and other
          $02  write   w for the owner

     A directory is searchable (x) wherever it is readable; a file
     is never executable.

     So an ordinary file ($C3 or $E3) is -rw-r--r--, a locked one
     ($01) -r--r--r--, and a directory drwxr-xr-x.  The destroy
     ($80) and rename ($40) bits have no file-mode counterpart and
     do not appear in the mode, but a file or directory whose
     destroy bit is clear cannot be removed, and one whose rename
     bit is clear cannot be renamed, even by the super-user
     (EPERM); moving it to another directory under the same name
     is allowed, as GS/OS allows it.  The backup bit ($20) and GS/OS's
     invisible bit ($04) are not permissions.  Every write,
     truncation and rename sets the backup bit, as ProDOS 8 does,
     so backup programs see the change.  The super-user reads and writes everything, as on any
     other file system.

     A new file or directory takes destroy, rename and backup,
     and read and write from the owner's bits of the mode it is
     created with: $E3 for 0644 or 0755.  A new file is a ProDOS
     file of type $00, as GS/OS creates one.

DATES
     A file's access, modification and change times are all its
     ProDOS modification date and time; ProDOS keeps no access
     time, and its other date is a creation date.  A directory's
     are those of its entry in its parent, and the volume
     directory's are its creation date.  A year value from 0 to 39
     is 2000 to 2039; any larger value is 1900 plus it, which is
     how GS/OS reads it.  Times are local, as FGX has no
     timezone.  An entry with no date reads as the epoch.

     A write sets the file's modification date and time and, when
     the file is closed, those of every directory above it up to
     the volume, as ProDOS 8 does.  A time outside 1940 to 2039 is
     stored as no date.

RENAMING
     A rename keeps the file's blocks, dates and access, and takes
     the case of its new name.  A name that already exists is
     replaced, as rename(2) requires, by removing it first, so its
     destroy bit must be set and a directory it names must be
     empty (ENOTEMPTY); neither ProDOS 8 nor GS/OS replaces a
     name.  A directory cannot be moved beneath itself (EINVAL).

TRUNCATING
     A file made longer allocates nothing: the new bytes read as
     zeros and take blocks when written.  A file made shorter gives
     back every block past its new end, and becomes a sapling or a
     seedling again when one holds it, as ProDOS 8's SET_EOF does.
     A file is at most 16777215 bytes (EFBIG).  A directory cannot
     be truncated (EISDIR).

SEE ALSO
     mount(2), mount(8), umount(8), df(1), ls(1)

BUGS
     A file's mode, owner and dates cannot be changed: chmod(2),
     chown(2) and utimes(2) fail with ENOTSUP.

     A tree whose first index block is a hole stays a tree when
     truncated to a length one index block could address.

     A removed file's blocks are given back when its last
     reference goes, so a file unlinked while it is open keeps
     working; if the system stops first, the blocks stay marked
     in use and nothing names them.

     The volume directory holds 51 entries and cannot grow; a
     create past them fails with ENOSPC, as ProDOS's does.

     A file's inode number is the block that holds its index, and
     that block moves when a file grows past 512 bytes or 128
     kilobytes: a growing file's inode number changes.  A
     directory's never does.

     Owner, group and mode cannot be chosen at mount time.

     A pathname longer than 63 characters is shortened by the
     system call layer rather than refused (apple/fgx#436); change
     directory part of the way first.



